TASK
Write the technical anatomy of THIS module only.

Rules:
- data_model entities must cover the spec's "data" list — each with its real key fields for THIS business.
- If the spec's "ai" is null, set "ai_agent" to null. Otherwise the agent's tools must map to actions the module's features actually need; its brain must be grounded in data THIS business really has (their services, their records, their policies) — never an invented vendor or dataset.
- model_tier is "fast" (cheap, high-volume: classification, extraction, routing, drafting) or "reasoning" (complex judgement) — choose per the work, and prefer "fast" unless the work truly needs judgement.
- guardrails are things the agent must NEVER do, specific to this business's risk (not "be safe").
- evaluation: how we would MEASURE this agent works before trusting it (offline checks, human review loops, thresholds).

THRESHOLD LAW: every accuracy target, SLA, evaluation window, sample size, rate or performance number in the spec, evaluation criteria or done-when checks is either a fact the client stated, or carries "(proposed — client approval required)". An unlabeled invented threshold is a defect.

IDENTIFIERS: every API path is a URL-safe slug — lowercase words joined by hyphens or underscores, parameters in {braces}, never a space ("/api/v1/pilot/opt_out/{customer_phone_number}", not "/api/v1/pilot/opt out").

FINANCIAL-DATA FLOOR: when this module touches payments, settlements, payouts or sensitive financial records, its security bullets MUST cover: authentication stronger than a phone number or order id for financial records — name the concrete method (a one-time code to the registered contact, a PIN, or sign-in through the client's existing account); "verify the WhatsApp number matches a known client" alone is a defect; per-client isolation (no cross-client exposure; end customers never see merchant settlement data); audit logging of reads and changes; manual named-person approval for financial adjustments with segregation of duties. Retrievable financial identifiers (bank accounts, payout details) are stored ENCRYPTED or tokenized with managed keys and masked in presentation — NEVER one-way hashed: a hash cannot execute a payout. Every client-scoped API authorizes from the AUTHENTICATED tenant context — a client identifier in the URL is never trusted by itself.

